   104	## 1. Design (a″_k): a two-dimensional random rational side
   105	
   106	Everything not mentioned here is as in hypD §1–§3: Dixon descent, Theorem DD, Theorems S/S′ and K, the
   107	field-selection rule and the LP shape test. Those results treat the field A abstractly and act only on
   108	R and A, so they apply verbatim to the field law below.
   109	
   110	**Notation.**
   111	- L = L_N[1/2, 1] and Λ = (log N log log N)^{1/2}.
   112	- s = 1/(d+1), M = ⌊N^s⌋, B = L[1/2, a], ϱ(x, y) = Ψ(x, y)/x, and 𝒮_B = the B-smooth integers (sign
   113	  ignored).
   114	- ℓ is uniform among the primes of J = (2^{j−1}, 2^j], with 2^{kj} ≍ √N, so ℓ = N^{1/(2k)+o(1)}.
   115	- S = L^σ with σ > 0 fixed (S² will be T_SQ up to L^{o(1)}), and V = ⌊√ℓ·S⌋.
   116	- H = N^h.
   117	
   118	**Preprocessing [standard].** Trial division up to B, at cost B·(log N)^{O(1)} = L^{a+o(1)}. This is
   119	below the final cost L^{c}, since c ≥ 2a. Afterwards N has no prime factor ≤ B, and is not a prime
   120	power. This replaces Lemma Ω for everything below; 2^ω = L^{o(1)} still holds.
   121	
   122	**Field law 𝒜.**
   123	- (g₀, g₁) is uniform on 𝒢 := {(g₀, g₁) ∈ Z² ∩ [M/2, M]² : gcd(g₀, g₁) = 1}, and G(x, y) := g₁x − g₀y.
   124	- c is uniform on C_H := [−H, H]^d ∩ Z^d, and R_c(x, y) := Σ_{i<d} c_i x^{d−1−i}y^i.
   125	- F_A := F̂_g + G·R_c, with F̂_g from Lemma GB. For P = (a, b):
   126	    F_A(P) = F̂_g(P) + G(P)·⟨c, v(P)⟩,  v(P) := (a^{d−1−i}b^i)_{0≤i<d}.
   127	- If gcd(g₁, N) > 1 or gcd(G(P), N) > 1, a factor of N has been found. Otherwise put
   128	  ψ: Z[x] → Z/N, x ↦ g₀g₁^{−1}.
   129	
   130	**Lemma GB (generalized base expansion) [proved].** Let g₁ ≥ 2 and gcd(g₀, g₁) = 1. Define
   131	T_d := N, and for i = d, d−1, …, 1:
   132	  a_i ≡ T_i g₀^{−i} (mod g₁), |a_i| ≤ g₁/2,  T_{i−1} := (T_i − a_i g₀^i)/g₁,
   133	and finally a_0 := T_0. Then F̂(g₀, g₁) := Σ_i a_i g₀^i g₁^{d−i} = N. If g₀, g₁ ∈ [M/2, M], then
   134	|a_0| ≤ 2^{d+1}M + d2^{d−1}M, so ‖f̂‖_∞ = O_d(M).
   135	*Proof.*
   136	1. T_i ≡ a_i g₀^i (mod g₁) by the choice of a_i, so T_{i−1} ∈ Z.
   137	2. By induction, T_i = Σ_{j≤i} a_j g₀^j g₁^{i−j}. At i = d this is the claim.
   138	3. For the bound, a_0 = (N − Σ_{i≥1} a_i g₀^i g₁^{d−i})/g₁^d. Here N/g₁^d ≤ 2^d N/M^d ≤ 2^{d+1}M, and
   139	   each |a_i|(g₀/g₁)^i ≤ (g₁/2)·2^i ≤ 2^{i−1}M. ∎
   140	(`src/pcest/geometry.py: gbe`; tested.)
   141	
   142	*Remarks.*
   143	- **Prior art.** The non-monic linear side g₁x − g₀ with a base-(g₀, g₁) expansion is Buhler–Lenstra–
   144	  Pomerance's base-(m₁, m₂) variant (LNM 1554 (1993) 50–94; Murphy, thesis, ANU 1999, §2.3.2). Lemma GB
   145	  is that construction with explicit bounds. The design is not new. What we did not find elsewhere is
   146	  its use to randomize the rational side in a rigorous pair-correlation argument (Theorem R).
   147	- **Kleinjung constraint.** Kleinjung (Math. Comp. 75, 2006) prescribes a small leading coefficient
   148	  a_d, and therefore needs a_d g₀^d ≡ N (mod g₁). Here a_d is *determined* by (g₀, g₁), so (g₀, g₁)
   149	  can be sampled freely subject only to coprimality. Codex makes the same point.
   150	- **Sizes.** The price is a_d ≈ g₁ rather than a small leading coefficient. That is irrelevant at the
   151	  exponent level, because LV's f̂ already has coefficients ≈ M.
   152	
   153	**Lemma 1.2 [proved].** For every P = (a, b), g₁^d F_A(P) ≡ N b^d (mod G(P)).
   154	*Proof.* g₁·P − b·(g₀, g₁) = (G(P), 0). Since F_A is an integral form, g₁^d F_A(P) = F_A(g₁P) ≡
   155	F_A(b·(g₀, g₁)) = b^d F_A(g₀, g₁) = b^d N, because G(g₀, g₁) = 0. ∎
   156	
   157	**Relations.** If G(P) = ±ℓn with n ∈ 𝒮_B and F_A(P) ∈ 𝒮_B, then
   158	  ψ(a − bα) = G(P)/g₁ = ±ℓn/g₁ (mod N).
   159	This is hypD's descent relation, multiplied by the per-field unit g₁^{−1}.
   160	- One extra column, the parity of the number of relations, makes g₁ a square in every dependency.
   161	  This is the same device LV and Buhler–Lenstra–Pomerance use for f_d.
   162	- **Irreducibility.** LV Lemma 5.1 transfers. Its Turán-sieve count uses only the affine structure
   163	  f ∈ f̂ + (x − m)·Z[x]_{d−1}, and the condition "g(m) ≡ n (mod r)" for the reductions g mod r. With
   164	  g₁x − g₀ in place of x − m, the condition becomes ĝ(g₀, g₁) ≡ N (mod r) for r ∤ g₁N. Claim 5.2
   165	  counts monic irreducibles with a prescribed value at an affine point m, here m = g₀g₁^{−1} mod r
   166	  (r ∤ g₁). It is unchanged.
   167	  [transfer of a published proof, checked at the level of its statements]
   168	- A reducible f either gives F₁(g₀, g₁)F₂(g₀, g₁) = N with both factors ≠ ±1, which is a factorization,
   169	  or is rare.
   170	
   171	**Counted candidates and the capped search.**
   172	- Put 𝒫_ℓ := {(a, b) : b prime, V/2 < b ≤ V, |a| ≤ V, gcd(a, b) = 1}, and
   173	  Λ_ℓ(g) := {P : ℓ | G(P)}, which has index ℓ when ℓ ∤ g₁.
   174	- The search enumerates Λ_ℓ(g) ∩ [−V, V]² from a Gauss-reduced basis, tests membership in 𝒫_ℓ, and
   175	  tests G(P)/ℓ and F_A(P) for B-smoothness.
   176	  - Primality of b uses a deterministic polynomial-time test (AKS).
   177	  - Smoothness is tested by HECM, as in LV Cor. 3.19.
   178	- N_ℓ(A) := #{P ∈ 𝒫_ℓ ∩ Λ_ℓ(g) : |G(P)| ≥ MV/32, G(P)/ℓ ∈ 𝒮_B, F_A(P) ∈ 𝒮_B}. Each such P is a
   179	  certified descent relation. ℓ² ∤ G(P) holds automatically, because ℓ > B.
   180	  - The **filter |G(P)| ≥ MV/32** was added after codex pcest_2 (§7.2). Without it, candidates with
   181	    |G| as small as ℓ have algebraic values smaller by a polynomial factor. Their smoothness density
   182	    can then exceed ρ_F by L^{Θ(1)}, which breaks Theorem 4.3's comparison. FM only ever uses
   183	    |G| ∈ [MV/32, 2MV], so the filter costs nothing there.
   184	  - A capped randomized smoothness test is repeated O(log N) times, so that a smooth value is
   185	    recognized with probability ≥ 1 − N^{−2}. This costs a polylogarithmic factor.
   186	- **Lemma 1.3 (regularity) [proved].** Suppose λ₁(Λ_ℓ(g)) < √ℓ/(2S), attained at w. Then
   187	  Λ_ℓ(g) ∩ 𝒫_ℓ = ∅. Otherwise #(Λ_ℓ(g) ∩ [−V, V]²) ≤ C_abs·S² for an absolute constant C_abs (e.g. 100).
   188	  *Proof.*
   189	  - w is primitive in Z²: its content e satisfies e ≤ |w| < ℓ, so gcd(e, ℓ) = 1 and w/e ∈ Λ_ℓ(g); by
   190	    minimality e = 1.
   191	  - So w is primitive in Λ_ℓ(g) (index ℓ), and the lattice is the union of the lines jw′ + Zw, j ∈ Z,
   192	    which are parallel to w at mutual distance ℓ/|w| > 2V.
   193	  - Every point of [−V, V]² has norm ≤ √2V < 2V, so it lies on the central line Zw. The only primitive
   194	    points there are ±w, and |w| < V/2 < b for every point of 𝒫_ℓ.
   195	  - In the regular case take a reduced basis (λ₁ ≤ λ₂, λ₁λ₂ ≤ (2/√3)ℓ). The translates of the
   196	    fundamental cell F at the lattice points of K = [−V, V]² are disjoint and lie in K + F. So the count
   197	    is ≤ (area(K) + perimeter(K)·diam F + π diam(F)²)/ℓ ≤ 4S² + O(V/λ₁) + O(λ₂/λ₁) ≤ C_abs S², using
   198	    λ₁ ≥ √ℓ/(2S), V/λ₁ ≤ 2S² and λ₂/λ₁ ≤ (2/√3)ℓ/λ₁² ≤ 8S²/√3. ∎
   199	- So the cap T_SQ := C_abs S²·(log N)^{O(1)} is never binding on a lattice that contains a candidate.
   200	  Irregular lattices need no separate exclusion: they contribute nothing.
   201	
   202	**Sizes [proved arithmetic].** For P ∈ 𝒫_ℓ and g ∈ 𝒢:
   203	- |G(P)| ≤ 2MV, so |G/ℓ| ≤ X_r := 2MV/ℓ = N^{s−1/(4k)}L^{O(1)};
   204	- |F̂_g(P)| = O_d(MV^d);
   205	- |G(P)⟨c, v(P)⟩| ≤ 2dMHV^d, so |F_A(P)| ≤ X_F := C_d MHV^d = N^{s+h+d/(4k)}L^{O(1)}.
   206	
   207	With ρ_r := ϱ(X_r, B) and ρ_F := ϱ(X_F, B), these are exactly hypD's exponents, so
   208	θ = 2s + h + (d−1)/(4k) as in `src/pcest/params.py`.
