   210	## 2. FM in full (regime (B), design (a″_k))
   211	
   212	### 2.1 Cited results, with their exact ranges
   213	
   214	Throughout, x = N^{ξ+o(1)} with ξ = s + h + d/(4k) fixed, y = B = L[1/2, a], and u = log x/log y ≍
   215	(log N/log log N)^{1/2}. So (log x)^K < y < x^{1/log log x} for any fixed K and large N.
   216	
   217	- **(H) Harper, arXiv 1208.5992; LV Facts 8.8–8.12.** The constants are absolute and effective.
   218	  - Fact 8.11 (Harper Prop. 2): for 0 < η < 1/80, y ≤ x^{9/10}, x^η ≤ Q ≤ √x and M₀ = x^η,
   219	      Σ_{M₀≤r≤Q} Σ_{χ*_r} Σ_{t∈[M₀,Q]} φ(t)^{−1} Σ_{χ_t induced by χ*_r} |Ψ(x, y; χ_t)|
   220	        ≪ log^{7/2}x · √Ψ(x, y)·(Q + x^{1/2−η} log²x).
   221	- **(LV8.5) LV Lemma 8.5 and its proof.** Fix ε > 0. For (log x)^K < y < x^{1/log log x},
   222	  x^ε ≤ Q ≤ √Ψ(x, y), ω₀ → ∞ and ω₀ = y^{O(1)}, the majorant
   223	    W(Q) := Σ_{t∈[Q/ω₀, Q], t y-smooth} φ(t)^{−1} Σ_{χ_t ≠ χ₀} |Ψ(x, y; χ_t)|
   224	  (LV eq. (8.2)) satisfies W(Q) ≪ Ψ(x, y)ϱ(Q, y)(H(u)^{−c} + y^{−c}) + Q√Ψ(x, y) log^{7/2}x, where
   225	  H(u) = e^{u/log²(u+1)}.
   226	  - LV's proof bounds W itself, not only the max-residue sum, which W majorizes.
   227	  - The constant is effective; Siegel and Page characters are treated in LV's G₁ part.
   228	- **(T) Tenenbaum (LV Fact 3.16, Cor. 3.17).** If ω(t) ≤ y^c/log(1 + u), then
   229	    Ψ_t(x, y) = (φ(t)/t)Ψ(x, y)(1 + O(log(1+u) log(1+ω(t))/log y))(1 + O(ω(t)/y)).
   230	  In our range the error is o(1) uniformly for t ≤ x, since ω(t) ≤ log x.
   231	- **(Hi) Hildebrand.**
   232	  - LV Fact 3.13: for log y ≥ (log log x)^{5/3+ε} and 1 ≤ z ≤ y^{5/12},
   233	      Ψ(x(1 + 1/z), y) − Ψ(x, y) = (Ψ(x, y)/z)(1 + O(log(u+1)/log y)).
   234	  - Ψ(2x, y) ≤ 2Ψ(x, y) (LV ref. [19, Thm 4]). Hence ϱ(z₂) ≤ 2ϱ(z₁) whenever z₂ ≥ z₁.
   235	- **(C8.16) LV Claim 8.16.** If u → ∞ and c ≥ 0 is fixed, then ϱ(x, y) = ϱ(xy^c, y)(log x)^{O(⌈c⌉)}.

 succeeded in 0ms:
   236	  So ϱ changes by (log N)^{O(1)} = L^{o(1)} when its argument moves by a factor y^{O(1)} = L^{O(1)}.
   237	- **(LV5) LV Lemmas 5.13, 5.18, 5.20.** These concern only the linear map φ_P(c) = ⟨c, v(P)⟩. They do
   238	  not involve m, so they transplant verbatim (§2.3, step 2).
   239	
   240	### 2.2 Lemma 8.5′ (restricted BV for moduli ℓ·(smooth)) [proved; regime (B)]
   241	
   242	**Statement.**
   243	- Let 0 < q < ξ/2 with q > 1/(2k) + η₁, for some fixed η₁ > 0, and Q = N^{q+o(1)}.
   244	- Let Y = 2^{j−1} and 𝒯_Q := {t = ℓs ∈ (Q/2, Q] : ℓ ∈ (Y, 2Y] prime, P⁺(s) ≤ B}.
   245	- Let δ_𝒯 := ϱ(Q/Y, B)/log Y. Then #𝒯_Q ≍ Qδ_𝒯.
   246	- Conclusion: Σ_{t∈𝒯_Q} max_{(r,t)=1} |Ψ(x, B; t, r) − Ψ_t(x, B)/φ(t)| ≪ Ψ(x, B)δ_𝒯·ε_N, where
   247	  ε_N := H(u)^{−c} + B^{−c} + x^{−η₂} for some fixed η₂ > 0.
   248	
   249	*Proof.*
   250	- **Setup.**
   251	  - #𝒯_Q = Σ_ℓ (Ψ(Q/ℓ) − Ψ(Q/2ℓ)).
   252	  - Fact 3.15 gives Ψ(z/2) = 2^{−α}Ψ(z)(1 + o(1)) with α ≥ 1/2, so each difference is ≍ Ψ(Q/ℓ).
   253	  - Summing gives #𝒯_Q ≍ Qδ_𝒯.
   254	  - Since ℓ > B ≥ P⁺(s), ℓ ∤ s and φ(t) = (ℓ − 1)φ(s). Every character mod t is χ_ℓχ_s, and t
   255	    determines (ℓ, s).
   256	  - The left side is at most W′ := Σ_{t∈𝒯_Q} φ(t)^{−1} Σ_{χ≠χ₀ mod t} |Ψ(x, B; χ)|.
   257	- **Part 1: χ_ℓ principal, χ_s ≠ χ₀.**
   258	  - Every B-smooth n is coprime to ℓ, so Ψ(x, B; χ₀^{(ℓ)}χ_s) = Ψ(x, B; χ_s).
   259	  - So this part equals Σ_ℓ (ℓ−1)^{−1} Σ_{s∈(Q/2ℓ, Q/ℓ], smooth} φ(s)^{−1} Σ_{χ_s≠χ₀} |Ψ(x, B; χ_s)|.
   260	  - By positivity this is ≤ Σ_ℓ (ℓ−1)^{−1} W(Q/ℓ), with ω₀ = log x.
   261	  - (LV8.5) applies with Q′ = Q/ℓ = N^{q−1/(2k)+o(1)} ≥ x^ε, where ε := η₁/(2ξ), and Q′ ≤ √Ψ.
   262	  - Hence Part 1 ≪ Ψ(H(u)^{−c} + B^{−c})·Σ_ℓ ϱ(Q/ℓ)/ℓ + Q√Ψ log^{7/2}x·Σ_ℓ ℓ^{−2}.
   263	  - The first sum is ≤ 2ϱ(Q/2Y)Σ_ℓ ℓ^{−1} ≪ δ_𝒯, using (Hi) and Mertens on (Y, 2Y].
   264	  - The second term is ≪ (Q/Y)√Ψ log^{7/2}x ≤ Ψδ_𝒯x^{−η₂}. This holds because Q ≤ x^{1/2−(ξ/2−q)/2ξ}
   265	    and Ψ, δ_𝒯 ≥ x^{−o(1)}.
   266	- **Part 2: χ_ℓ non-principal.**
   267	  - The conductor is ℓ·r′ ≥ Y = N^{1/(2k)+o(1)}.
   268	  - Choose η := min(1/100, 1/(4kξ)). Then Y ≥ x^η = M₀, so all these characters have conductor in
   269	    [M₀, Q], with moduli t ∈ (Q/2, Q] ⊂ [M₀, Q].
   270	  - By positivity, Part 2 is at most the left side of Fact 8.11, which is
   271	      ≪ log^{7/2}x √Ψ (Q + x^{1/2−η} log²x) ≤ Ψδ_𝒯 x^{−η₂},
   272	    using q < ξ/2 and Ψ, δ_𝒯 ≥ x^{−o(1)}.
   273	- The principal character of t contributes exactly Ψ_t(x)/φ(t), which is the subtracted main term. ∎
   274	
   275	*Remarks.*
   276	- Unlike hypD §4.1′, no conductor range [Y, M₀] arises in regime (B), so neither Fact 8.9 nor Fact 8.10
   277	  is needed for χ_ℓ ≠ χ₀.
   278	- ε_N → 0 uniformly in N, and ε_N·(log L)^{O(1)} → 0, since H(u)^{−c} = exp(−c(log N)^{1/2}/
   279	  (log log N)^{5/2+o(1)}).
