   398	## 3. PC_rat: Theorem R
   399	
   400	Throughout this section Y_P := 1[G(P)/ℓ ∈ 𝒮_B] is the rational event for a candidate P. The rational
   401	counts are
   402	  R1 := E_{ℓ,g} Σ_{P∈𝒫_ℓ} 1[P ∈ Λ_ℓ(g)]Y_P,
   403	  R2 := E_{ℓ,g} Σ_{P≠P′∈𝒫_ℓ} 1[P, P′ ∈ Λ_ℓ(g)]Y_PY_{P′}.
   404	
   405	**Lemma Q (pair image lattice) [proved; tested].**
   406	- Let P = (a, b) ≠ P′ = (a′, b′) be in 𝒫_ℓ with Δ := ab′ − a′b ≡ 0 (mod ℓ). Δ ≠ 0, since b, b′ > 0 and
   407	  both points are primitive.
   408	- Then L_ℓ(P) := {g ∈ Z² : ℓ | G_g(P)} = L_ℓ(P′).
   409	- The map g ↦ (n, n′) := (G_g(P)/ℓ, G_g(P′)/ℓ) is injective on L_ℓ(P), and its image is
   410	    Γ_q = {(n, n′) ∈ Z² : n′ ≡ λn (mod q)},  q := |Δ|/ℓ ≤ 2S²,  λ :≡ b′b^{−1} (mod q).
   411	- Since b is a prime > V/2 > 2S² ≥ q, b is invertible mod q, and λ is a unit.
   412	
   413	*Proof.*
   414	1. g ↦ (G_g(P), G_g(P′)) is the integer matrix [[a, −b], [a′, −b′]] acting on (g₁, g₀). Its determinant
   415	   is −Δ ≠ 0, so the map is injective with image of index |Δ|.
   416	2. L_ℓ(P) has index ℓ in Z². ℓ | Δ forces ℓ | G_g(P′) whenever ℓ | G_g(P), so L_ℓ(P) = L_ℓ(P′), and its
   417	   image lies in ℓZ² with index ℓ|Δ| in Z². Dividing by ℓ gives a lattice of index ℓ|Δ|/ℓ² = q.
   418	3. b·G_g(P′) − b′·G_g(P) = g₁(ba′ − b′a) = −g₁Δ, so bn′ − b′n = ∓g₁q ≡ 0 (mod q). The image therefore
   419	   lies in Γ_q, which also has index q, so the two are equal. ∎
   420	- This matches codex pcest_1 (3). The index is q, not ℓq: the special-q condition is absorbed by the
   421	  division by ℓ.
   422	
   423	**Lemma DS (divisor switching: smooth numbers in a short interval and a progression) [proved;
   424	elementary].**
   425	- Setup: let C₀ ≥ 1 be fixed, y → ∞ with y ≥ (log X)^K, u = log X/log y → ∞, and 1 ≤ q ≤ y^{C₀}. Let
   426	  I ⊂ [X y^{−C₀}, X] be an interval of length A ≥ X y^{−C₀}.
   427	- Conclusion: for every r ∈ Z,
   428	    #{n ∈ I : n ≡ r (mod q), P⁺(n) ≤ y} ≤ (A/q)·ϱ(X, y)·(log X)^{O_{C₀}(1)}.
   429	
   430	*Proof.*
   431	1. Let e := gcd(r, q). If e has a prime factor > y, the count is 0. Otherwise write n = en₁. Then
   432	   n₁ ∈ I/e has length A/e and n₁ ≡ r/e (mod q′) with q′ = q/e and gcd(r/e, q′) = 1, so n₁ is coprime
   433	   to q′.
   434	2. Put D := min(A/q, X/(e y^{C₀})). Then D ≤ min(I/e).
   435	3. Every y-smooth n₁ ≥ D has a divisor d with D/y < d ≤ D: take the largest product of its smallest
   436	   prime factors that is ≤ D.
   437	4. Write n₁ = dm. Then gcd(d, q′) = 1, m ≡ (r/e)d^{−1} (mod q′), and m lies in an interval of length
   438	   A/(ed). So there are ≤ A/(dq) + 1 ≤ 2A/(dq) choices of m, since A/(Dq) ≥ 1.
   439	5. Hence the count is ≤ (2A/q) Σ_{D/y<d≤D, P⁺(d)≤y} 1/d.
   440	6. By dyadic blocks and (Hi), the sum is ≤ 8 log₂(2y)·ϱ(D/y, y).
   441	7. Finally X/(D/y) ≤ y^{2C₀+1}, so (C8.16) gives ϱ(D/y, y) ≤ ϱ(X, y)(log X)^{O(C₀)}. ∎
   442	*Attribution.*
   443	- The case q = 1 follows from Hildebrand's subadditivity Ψ(x + z, y) ≤ Ψ(x, y) + Ψ(z, y) (Quart. J.
   444	  Math. 36 (1985) 57–69) together with (C8.16).
   445	- Choosing a divisor in (D/y, D] is the standard flexible factorization of smooth numbers (Friedlander,
   446	  Acta Arith. 39 (1981); Vaughan's iterative method).
   447	- Codex pcest_2 points to Harper, Compositio 152 (2016), §2.1, "Smooth Numbers Result 3", as a stronger
   448	  published progression version, including non-coprime residues. We have not checked its exact ranges
   449	  against ours, so we use our own proof above.
   450	- Lemma DS is therefore a progression extension of known short-interval bounds, not a new result.
   451	- Codex's blind round states the same bound (its (1)).
   452	
   453	**Theorem R (rational pair correlation) [proved].** In design (a″_k), with B = L[1/2, a] and S = L^σ
   454	satisfying S² ≥ ρ_r^{−2}:
   455	  R2 ≤ (log N)^{O(1)}·R1²  and  R1 ≥ S²ρ_r/(log N)^{O(1)}.
   456	
   457	*Proof.*
   458	- **The lower bound on R1** is Steps 1 and 4 of §2.3 without the algebraic factor.
   459	- **The pair count.** Fix ℓ and a pair (P, P′) as in Lemma Q. Then
   460	    #{g ∈ 𝒢 ∩ L_ℓ(P) : Y_PY_{P′} = 1} ≤ #{(n, n′) ∈ Γ_q ∩ Π : n, n′ ∈ 𝒮_B}.
   461	  Here Π := ℓ^{−1}[[a, −b], [a′, −b′]]·[M/2, M]² is a parallelogram of area M²q/(4ℓ), contained in
   462	  [−X_r, X_r]².
   463	- **Shape of Π.** One side is (M/2ℓ)(b, b′), of length ≍ MV/ℓ and with direction in
   464	  [atan(1/2), atan 2], because b, b′ ∈ (V/2, V]. So Π has width ≥ area/(2·long side) ≥ Mq/(8√2V) ≥
   465	  w₀/12, where w₀ := M/V.
   466	- **Covering.** Cover Π by the grid squares of side w₀ that meet it. There are ≤ C·area(Π)/w₀² = C·qS²
   467	  of them.
   468	- **A square away from the axes.** Let 𝒬 = I₁ × I₂ have |n|, |n′| ≥ X_r S^{−2} throughout.
   469	  - Lemma DS applies to each coordinate (sign symmetric; I_i ⊂ [X_rS^{−2}, X_r];
   470	    A = w₀ = X_r/(2S²) ≥ X_r y^{−C₀}; q ≤ 2S² ≤ y^{C₀}).

