   219	## 3. Theorem K: bounded-k splits reduce Hypothesis D to one averaged statement
   220	
   221	**Setup.**
   222	- Fix integers k ≥ 1 and odd d ≥ 3. Let X = ⌊√(N/2)⌋.
   223	- Let j be maximal with 2^{kj} ≤ X, and put J = (2^{j−1}, 2^j], so ℓ ∈ J means ℓ = N^{1/(2k)+o(1)}.
   224	- For a field A, let G_A ⊂ J be the set of primes ℓ ∤ N for which a fixed, deterministic,
   225	  capped special-q search succeeds. The search depends only on (A, ℓ), with time cap T_SQ·L^{o(1)}. It
   226	  succeeds when it finds a primitive (a, b) ∈ Λ_ℓ(m) in the box with (a − bm)/ℓ and F_A(a, b) both
   227	  B-smooth.
   228	- Let g_A = |G_A|/π_J with π_J = #(primes in J), and γ := E_A g_A = Pr_{A, ℓ∈J}[ℓ ∈ G_A].
   229	
   230	**Hypothesis D_k(γ).** γ ≥ L^{−o(1)}, uniformly in N. The search box and the field law are those of §4.
   231	
   232	**Theorem K [proved, given the setup].**
   233	- (i) Pr_A[g_A ≥ γ/2] ≥ γ/2.
   234	- (ii) Fix a field A with |G_A| ≥ 4k², and let S_A be the products of exactly k distinct primes of
   235	  G_A. The hypothesis on |G_A| was missing; codex fix. Then for N ≥ N₀(k),
   236	    Pr_R[R ≡ u/v with u, v ∈ S_A] ≥ c_k·(g_A/(4 log N))^{4k}·2^{−ω}/(1 + log N), with c_k > 0 depending
   237	    only on k.
   238	- (iii) **[corrected after review]**
   239	  - Acceptance rule (codex fix). Accept R when the primitive split (u₀, v₀) has each side a product of
   240	    **at most** k distinct primes of G_A. "Exactly k" is not divisor-closed: cancelling gcd(u, v) can
   241	    remove primes.
   242	  - The count in (ii) still lower-bounds acceptance, because the primitive part of an S_A-pair is
   243	    accepted.
   244	  - In a field with g_A ≥ γ/2 and |G_A| ≥ 4k², a descent attempt succeeds after an expected
   245	    L^{o(1)}·γ^{−2k} R-trials.
   246	  - Each trial must split u₀, v₀ ≈ √N into their prime factors, all of which are ≈ N^{1/(2k)}. This is
   247	    general factoring of √N-size integers, **not** a smoothness test.
   248	  - HECM would cost L_{N^{1/(2k)}}[2/3] = L_N[2/3, c(2k)^{−2/3}]. The first version wrote "L_N[1/3]",
   249	    which is wrong.
   250	  - We use Lenstra–Pomerance's rigorous algorithm instead, at T_shape = L_{√N}[1/2, 1] = L_N[1/2, 1/√2]
   251	    per trial.
   252	  - Each trial with the right shape adds at most 2k special-q searches.
   253	- So the expected cost per descent relation is (T_shape + T_SQ)·L^{o(1)} whenever γ ≥ L^{−o(1)}.
