   116	- S = L^σ with σ > 0 fixed (S² will be T_SQ up to L^{o(1)}), and V = ⌊√ℓ·S⌋.
   117	- H = N^h.
   118	
   119	**Preprocessing [standard].** Trial division up to B, at cost B·(log N)^{O(1)} = L^{a+o(1)}. This is
   120	below the final cost L^{c}, since c ≥ 2a. Afterwards N has no prime factor ≤ B, and is not a prime
   121	power. This replaces Lemma Ω for everything below; 2^ω = L^{o(1)} still holds.
   122	
   123	**Field law 𝒜.**
   124	- (g₀, g₁) is uniform on 𝒢 := {(g₀, g₁) ∈ Z² ∩ [M/2, M]² : gcd(g₀, g₁) = 1}, and G(x, y) := g₁x − g₀y.
   125	- c is uniform on C_H := [−H, H]^d ∩ Z^d, and R_c(x, y) := Σ_{i<d} c_i x^{d−1−i}y^i.
   126	- F_A := F̂_g + G·R_c, with F̂_g from Lemma GB. For P = (a, b):
   127	    F_A(P) = F̂_g(P) + G(P)·⟨c, v(P)⟩,  v(P) := (a^{d−1−i}b^i)_{0≤i<d}.
   128	- If gcd(g₁, N) > 1 or gcd(G(P), N) > 1, a factor of N has been found. Otherwise put
   129	  ψ: Z[x] → Z/N, x ↦ g₀g₁^{−1}. It is well defined on Z[x]/(f): g₁^d f(g₀/g₁) = F(g₀, g₁) = N ≡ 0
   130	  (mod N).
   131	- F_A(P) = 0 is excluded from the counts.
   132	
   133	**Lemma GB (generalized base expansion) [proved].** Let g₁ ≥ 2 and gcd(g₀, g₁) = 1. Define
   134	T_d := N, and for i = d, d−1, …, 1:
   135	  a_i ≡ T_i g₀^{−i} (mod g₁), |a_i| ≤ g₁/2,  T_{i−1} := (T_i − a_i g₀^i)/g₁,
   136	and finally a_0 := T_0. Then F̂(g₀, g₁) := Σ_i a_i g₀^i g₁^{d−i} = N. If g₀, g₁ ∈ [M/2, M], then
   137	|a_0| ≤ 2^{d+1}M + d2^{d−1}M, so ‖f̂‖_∞ = O_d(M).
   138	*Proof.*
   139	1. T_i ≡ a_i g₀^i (mod g₁) by the choice of a_i, so T_{i−1} ∈ Z.
   140	2. By induction, T_i = Σ_{j≤i} a_j g₀^j g₁^{i−j}. At i = d this is the claim.
   141	3. For the bound, a_0 = (N − Σ_{i≥1} a_i g₀^i g₁^{d−i})/g₁^d. Here N/g₁^d ≤ 2^d N/M^d ≤ 2^{d+1}M, and
   142	   each |a_i|(g₀/g₁)^i ≤ (g₁/2)·2^i ≤ 2^{i−1}M. ∎
   143	(`src/pcest/geometry.py: gbe`; tested.)
   144	
   145	*Remarks.*
   146	- **Prior art.** The non-monic linear side g₁x − g₀ with a base-(g₀, g₁) expansion is Buhler–Lenstra–
   147	  Pomerance's base-(m₁, m₂) variant (LNM 1554 (1993) 50–94; Murphy, thesis, ANU 1999, §2.3.2). Lemma GB
   148	  is that construction with explicit bounds. The design is not new. What we did not find elsewhere is
   149	  its use to randomize the rational side in a rigorous pair-correlation argument (Theorem R).
   150	- **Kleinjung constraint.** Kleinjung (Math. Comp. 75, 2006) prescribes a small leading coefficient
   151	  a_d, and therefore needs a_d g₀^d ≡ N (mod g₁). Here a_d is *determined* by (g₀, g₁), so (g₀, g₁)
   152	  can be sampled freely subject only to coprimality. Codex makes the same point.
   153	- **Sizes.** The price is a_d ≈ g₁ rather than a small leading coefficient. That is irrelevant at the
   154	  exponent level, because LV's f̂ already has coefficients ≈ M.
   155	
   156	**Lemma 1.2 [proved].** For every P = (a, b), g₁^d F_A(P) ≡ N b^d (mod G(P)).
   157	*Proof.* g₁·P − b·(g₀, g₁) = (G(P), 0). Since F_A is an integral form, g₁^d F_A(P) = F_A(g₁P) ≡
   158	F_A(b·(g₀, g₁)) = b^d F_A(g₀, g₁) = b^d N, because G(g₀, g₁) = 0. ∎
   159	
   160	**Relations.** If G(P) = ±ℓn with n ∈ 𝒮_B and F_A(P) ∈ 𝒮_B, then
   161	  ψ(a − bα) = G(P)/g₁ = ±ℓn/g₁ (mod N).
   162	This is hypD's descent relation, multiplied by the per-field unit g₁^{−1}.
   163	- One extra column, the parity of the number of relations, makes g₁ a square in every dependency.
   164	  This is the same device LV and Buhler–Lenstra–Pomerance use for f_d.
   165	- **Irreducibility.** LV Lemma 5.1 transfers. Its Turán-sieve count uses only the affine structure
   166	  f ∈ f̂ + (x − m)·Z[x]_{d−1}, and the condition "g(m) ≡ n (mod r)" for the reductions g mod r. With
   167	  g₁x − g₀ in place of x − m, the condition becomes ĝ(g₀, g₁) ≡ N (mod r) for r ∤ g₁N. Claim 5.2
   168	  counts monic irreducibles with a prescribed value at an affine point m, here m = g₀g₁^{−1} mod r
   169	  (r ∤ g₁). It is unchanged.
   170	  [transfer of a published proof, checked at the level of its statements]
   171	  - a_d ≠ 0 in Lemma GB, because a_d ≡ N g₀^{−d} (mod g₁) and gcd(g₁, N) = 1.
   172	- A reducible f either gives F₁(g₀, g₁)F₂(g₀, g₁) = N with both factors ≠ ±1, which is a factorization,
   173	  or is rare.
   174	- **Quantified (Opus proof critic).** LV's bound transplants as Pr(f reducible) ≤ H^{−1/3+o(1)}. Field
   175	  selection keeps a field with law ∝ q_A, which amplifies rare events by at most 1/μ. So
   176	  Pr(kept field reducible) ≤ H^{−1/3+o(1)}/μ = N^{−h/3+o(1)}. The degree-drop event
   177	  a_d + g₁c₀ = 0 has probability ≤ 1/(2H + 1). Reducible fields are rejected.
   178	  - Status: transfer of LV Lemma 5.1, checked at the level of statements. It is listed among the
   179	    assumptions of Theorem A.
