   220	## 2. FM in full (regime (B), design (a″_k))
   221	
   222	### 2.1 Cited results, with their exact ranges
   223	
   224	Throughout, x = N^{ξ+o(1)} with ξ = s + h + d/(4k) fixed, y = B = L[1/2, a], and u = log x/log y ≍
   225	(log N/log log N)^{1/2}. So (log x)^K < y < x^{1/log log x} for any fixed K and large N.
   226	
   227	- **(H) Harper, arXiv 1208.5992; LV Facts 8.8–8.12.** The constants are absolute and effective.
   228	  - Fact 8.11 (Harper Prop. 2): for 0 < η < 1/80, y ≤ x^{9/10}, x^η ≤ Q ≤ √x and M₀ = x^η,
   229	      Σ_{M₀≤r≤Q} Σ_{χ*_r} Σ_{t∈[M₀,Q]} φ(t)^{−1} Σ_{χ_t induced by χ*_r} |Ψ(x, y; χ_t)|
   230	        ≪ log^{7/2}x · √Ψ(x, y)·(Q + x^{1/2−η} log²x).
   231	- **(LV8.5) LV Lemma 8.5 and its proof.** Fix ε > 0. For (log x)^K < y < x^{1/log log x},
   232	  x^ε ≤ Q ≤ √Ψ(x, y), ω₀ → ∞ and ω₀ = y^{O(1)}, the majorant
   233	    W(Q) := Σ_{t∈[Q/ω₀, Q], t y-smooth} φ(t)^{−1} Σ_{χ_t ≠ χ₀} |Ψ(x, y; χ_t)|
   234	  (LV eq. (8.2)) satisfies W(Q) ≪ Ψ(x, y)ϱ(Q, y)(H(u)^{−c} + y^{−c}) + Q√Ψ(x, y) log^{7/2}x, where
   235	  H(u) = e^{u/log²(u+1)}.

 succeeded in 0ms:
   236	  - LV's proof bounds W itself, not only the max-residue sum, which W majorizes.
   237	  - The constant is effective; Siegel and Page characters are treated in LV's G₁ part.
   238	- **(T) Tenenbaum (LV Fact 3.16, Cor. 3.17).** If ω(t) ≤ y^c/log(1 + u), then
   239	    Ψ_t(x, y) = (φ(t)/t)Ψ(x, y)(1 + O(log(1+u) log(1+ω(t))/log y))(1 + O(ω(t)/y)).
   240	  In our range the error is o(1) uniformly for t ≤ x, since ω(t) ≤ log x.
   241	- **(Hi) Hildebrand.**
   242	  - LV Fact 3.13: for log y ≥ (log log x)^{5/3+ε} and 1 ≤ z ≤ y^{5/12},
   243	      Ψ(x(1 + 1/z), y) − Ψ(x, y) = (Ψ(x, y)/z)(1 + O(log(u+1)/log y)).
   244	  - Ψ(2x, y) ≤ 2Ψ(x, y) (LV ref. [19, Thm 4]). Hence ϱ(z₂) ≤ 2ϱ(z₁) whenever z₂ ≥ z₁.
   245	- **(C8.16) LV Claim 8.16.** If u → ∞ and c ≥ 0 is fixed, then ϱ(x, y) = ϱ(xy^c, y)(log x)^{O(⌈c⌉)}.
   246	  So ϱ changes by (log N)^{O(1)} = L^{o(1)} when its argument moves by a factor y^{O(1)} = L^{O(1)}.
   247	- **(LV5) LV Lemmas 5.13, 5.18, 5.20.** These concern only the linear map φ_P(c) = ⟨c, v(P)⟩. They do
   248	  not involve m, so they transplant verbatim (§2.3, step 2).
   249	
   250	### 2.2 Lemma 8.5′ (restricted BV for moduli ℓ·(smooth)) [proved; regime (B)]
   251	
   252	**Statement.**
   253	- Let 0 < q < ξ/2 with q > 1/(2k) + η₁, for some fixed η₁ > 0, and Q = N^{q+o(1)}.
   254	- Let Y = 2^{j−1} and 𝒯_Q := {t = ℓs ∈ (Q/2, Q] : ℓ ∈ (Y, 2Y] prime, P⁺(s) ≤ B}.
   255	- Let δ_𝒯 := ϱ(Q/Y, B)/log Y. Then #𝒯_Q ≍ Qδ_𝒯.
   256	- Conclusion: Σ_{t∈𝒯_Q} max_{(r,t)=1} |Ψ(x, B; t, r) − Ψ_t(x, B)/φ(t)| ≪ Ψ(x, B)δ_𝒯·ε_N, where
   257	  ε_N := H(u)^{−c} + B^{−c} + x^{−η₂} for some fixed η₂ > 0.
   258	
   259	*Proof.*
   260	- **Setup.**
   261	  - #𝒯_Q = Σ_ℓ (Ψ(Q/ℓ) − Ψ(Q/2ℓ)).
   262	  - Fact 3.15 gives Ψ(z/2) = 2^{−α}Ψ(z)(1 + o(1)) with α ≥ 1/2, so each difference is ≍ Ψ(Q/ℓ).
   263	  - Summing gives #𝒯_Q ≍ Qδ_𝒯.
   264	  - Since ℓ > B ≥ P⁺(s), ℓ ∤ s and φ(t) = (ℓ − 1)φ(s). Every character mod t is χ_ℓχ_s, and t
   265	    determines (ℓ, s).
   266	  - The left side is at most W′ := Σ_{t∈𝒯_Q} φ(t)^{−1} Σ_{χ≠χ₀ mod t} |Ψ(x, B; χ)|.
   267	- **Part 1: χ_ℓ principal, χ_s ≠ χ₀.**
   268	  - Every B-smooth n is coprime to ℓ, so Ψ(x, B; χ₀^{(ℓ)}χ_s) = Ψ(x, B; χ_s).
   269	  - So this part equals Σ_ℓ (ℓ−1)^{−1} Σ_{s∈(Q/2ℓ, Q/ℓ], smooth} φ(s)^{−1} Σ_{χ_s≠χ₀} |Ψ(x, B; χ_s)|.
   270	  - By positivity this is ≤ Σ_ℓ (ℓ−1)^{−1} W(Q/ℓ), with ω₀ = log x.
   271	  - (LV8.5) applies with Q′ = Q/ℓ = N^{q−1/(2k)+o(1)} ≥ x^ε, where ε := η₁/(2ξ), and Q′ ≤ √Ψ.
   272	  - Hence Part 1 ≪ Ψ(H(u)^{−c} + B^{−c})·Σ_ℓ ϱ(Q/ℓ)/ℓ + Q√Ψ log^{7/2}x·Σ_ℓ ℓ^{−2}.
   273	  - The first sum is ≤ 2ϱ(Q/2Y)Σ_ℓ ℓ^{−1} ≪ δ_𝒯, using (Hi) and Mertens on (Y, 2Y].
   274	  - The second term is ≪ (Q/Y)√Ψ log^{7/2}x ≤ Ψδ_𝒯x^{−η₂}. This holds because Q ≤ x^{1/2−(ξ/2−q)/2ξ}
   275	    and Ψ, δ_𝒯 ≥ x^{−o(1)}.
   276	- **Part 2: χ_ℓ non-principal.**
   277	  - The conductor is ℓ·r′ ≥ Y = N^{1/(2k)+o(1)}.
   278	  - Choose η := min(1/100, 1/(4kξ)). Then Y ≥ x^η = M₀, so all these characters have conductor in
   279	    [M₀, Q], with moduli t ∈ (Q/2, Q] ⊂ [M₀, Q].
   280	  - By positivity, Part 2 is at most the left side of Fact 8.11, which is
   281	      ≪ log^{7/2}x √Ψ (Q + x^{1/2−η} log²x) ≤ Ψδ_𝒯 x^{−η₂},
   282	    using q < ξ/2 and Ψ, δ_𝒯 ≥ x^{−o(1)}.
   283	- The principal character of t contributes exactly Ψ_t(x)/φ(t), which is the subtracted main term. ∎
   284	
   285	*Remarks.*
   286	- Unlike hypD §4.1′, no conductor range [Y, M₀] arises in regime (B), so neither Fact 8.9 nor Fact 8.10
   287	  is needed for χ_ℓ ≠ χ₀.
   288	- Inherited from LV's proof: the Page-character step absorbs (log x)^{O(1)} into exp(−cu/log²u), which
   289	  needs u/log²u ≫ log log x. That holds in regime (B), where u ≍ (log N/log log N)^{1/2}, but not at
   290	  the edge of LV's stated range. We use Lemma 8.5′ only in regime (B).
   291	- In the setup, Fact 3.15 is used with α → 1. Ψ(2x, y) ≤ 2Ψ(x, y) can equally be taken from Fact 3.15,
   292	  up to a factor 1 + o(1).
   293	- ε_N → 0 uniformly in N, and ε_N·(log L)^{O(1)} → 0, since H(u)^{−c} = exp(−c(log N)^{1/2}/
   294	  (log log N)^{5/2+o(1)}).
   295	
   296	### 2.3 Theorem FM [proved]
   297	
   298	**Statement.**
   299	- Fix odd d ≥ 3, k > (d+1)/4, a > 0, σ > 0 and η₀ > 0, and let h ≥ max(1/(4k), s − (d−2)/(4k)) + η₀.
   300	- Then for all large N (preprocessed), with an implied constant depending only on (d, k, a, σ, h, η₀):
   301	    E_{ℓ∈J, A∼𝒜} N_ℓ(A) ≥ S²ρ_rρ_F/(log N)^{O(1)}.
   302	
   303	*Proof.* Write Pr_A for g uniform on 𝒢 and c uniform on C_H, independently. Then
   304	  E N = π_J^{−1} Σ_{ℓ∈J} Σ_{P∈𝒫_ℓ} Σ_{n∈𝒮_B} (|𝒢_P(n)|/|𝒢|)·E_{g∈𝒢_P(n)} Pr_c[F_A(P) ∈ 𝒮_B],
   305	where 𝒢_P(n) := {g ∈ 𝒢 : G_g(P) = ℓn}. Throughout, (log)^{O(1)} denotes (log N)^{O(1)}.
   306	
   307	**Step 1 (fibres and the induced law; hypD item 2).** Fix ℓ and P = (a, b) ∈ 𝒫_ℓ, and put V′ :=
   308	max(|a|, b) ∈ (V/2, V].
   309	- (i) The g with G_g(P) = ℓn lie on a line with direction (a, b) and point spacing |P|₂ ≥ V/2. The
   310	  square has chords of length ≤ M/√2. So |𝒢_P(n)| ≤ √2M/V + 1 for every n.
   311	- (ii) Consider the continuous model (g₀, g₁) uniform on [M/2, M]².
   312	  - G = g₁a − g₀b is a sum of independent uniforms on intervals of lengths |a|M/2 and bM/2.
   313	  - Its density is ≥ 1/(MV′) on a central interval of length ≥ MV′/2 ≥ MV/4.
   314	  - Removing |G| < MV/32 leaves an interval I*_P of G-values, of length ≥ MV/16, on which
   315	    |G| ∈ [MV/32, 2MV] and G has constant sign.
   316	  - For t ∈ I*_P the chord {G = t} has length ≥ (M²/4)|P|₂/(MV′) ≥ M/4, so it carries ≥ M/(8V)
   317	    lattice points for large N.
   318	  - Put I_P := I*_P/ℓ, a set of n-values of length ≥ MV/(16ℓ).
   319	- (iii) **Coprimality.** The integer points of the line g = g* + j(a, b) on which e | gcd(g₀, g₁) form
   320	  one residue class of j mod e if e | ℓn, and none otherwise, because gcd(a, b) = 1. By Möbius
   321	  inversion, for n ∈ I_P,
   322	    |𝒢_P(n)| ≥ (φ(ℓn)/ℓn)·M/(8V) − 2^{ω(ℓn)} ≥ M/(C V log log N),
   323	  since 2^{ω(ℓn)} ≤ N^{o(1)} ≪ M/V.
   324	
   325	**Step 2 (the algebraic side; hypD items 3, 4).** Fix ℓ, P, n ∈ I_P ∩ 𝒮_B and g ∈ 𝒢_P(n). Put
   326	t := |G(P)| = ℓ|n| and r := F̂_g(P) mod t.
   327	- (a) **(r, t) = 1, so the moving residue is reduced (item 3).**
   328	  - gcd(G, b) = gcd(g₁, b), and g₁a ≡ ℓn (mod b). Since b is a prime > B ≥ P⁺(n) and b ≠ ℓ (b ≤ V < ℓ),
   329	    gcd(g₁, b) = 1.
   330	  - gcd(G, g₁) = gcd(g₀b, g₁) = 1.
   331	  - Lemma 1.2 then gives gcd(r, t) = gcd(N, t) = 1, by preprocessing and ℓ ∤ N.
   332	  - The residue r moves with g and b. It enters only through max_r below.
   333	- (b) **Kernel and ϑ (LV 5.13, 5.18; item 4).**
   334	  - LV's induction in Lemma 5.13 uses only gcd(a, b) = 1. Exchanging the roles of a and b when
   335	    |a| > b, and absorbing signs into the x_i, it gives S_P ⊂ [−2V′, 2V′]^d on which φ_P is a
   336	    bijection onto an interval of length V′^{d−1}. It also gives the sub-kernels ν_l with
   337	    φ_Pν_l = Unif(I(l)).
   338	  - As in Lemma 5.18, with μ = Unif(C_H) and ν = μ ⋆ (⋆_i ν_{l_i}), l_i = |a|^{d−1−i}b^i:
   339	    ‖μ − ν‖₁ ≤ 2·Pr_μ[dist(c, ∂C_H) ≤ 4dV′] ≤ 8d²V/H.
   340	  - Also φ_Pν = ϑ, a convolution of d uniforms on intervals of lengths 2Hl_i, shifted by
   341	    |M₀| ≤ dV^{d−1}.
   342	  - Its density is ≤ (2H max_i l_i)^{−1} = (2HV′^{d−1})^{−1}, and its support lies in
   343	    [M₀ − T′, M₀ + T′] with T′ ≤ dHV^{d−1}.
   344	- (c) **Truncation (LV 5.20; item 4).** Fix ω := L^{C₁}, where C₁ exceeds the exponent of 1/ρ_F.
   345	  - Put Y_ω := (2H + 1)V′^{d−1}/ω. Let ϑ′ be LV's **flattening** of ϑ on the central part:
   346	    ϑ′(z) = ϑ(max(z, Y_ω)) for z ≥ 0 and ϑ(min(z, −Y_ω)) for z < 0. Nothing is deleted. Discrete
   347	    interval lengths are 2H + 1 rather than 2H; this changes only constants.
   348	  - Then ‖ϑ − ϑ′‖₁ ≤ 2/ω.
   349	  - ϑ′ is monotone away from 0, since Y_ω > |M₀| when H > dωV. So ϑ′ = Σ_{y≥Y_ω} (W_y Unif[0, y] +
   350	    W_{−y} Unif[−y, 0)), with W ≥ 0 and |1 − ΣW| ≤ 2/ω.
   351	- (d) **One uniform piece.**
   352	  - Let y ∈ [Y_ω, 2T′]. The values F̂ + G·m, m ∈ [0, y], form a progression ≡ r (mod t).
   353	  - **Sign correction (codex pcest_2).** If G and F̂ have opposite signs, the progression can cross 0;
   354	    its far endpoint then has absolute value ty − |F̂|, not ty. In every case, all terms except at most
   355	    2(|F̂|/t + 1) of them have one sign and absolute value in (|F̂|, ty − |F̂|]. This interval contains
   356	    (ty/4, ty/2], because |F̂| ≤ ty/4.
   357	  - Let X_F^max := C_d MH V_max^d with V_max := ⌊√(2Y)·S⌋, so it is common to all ℓ ∈ J. Let
   358	    𝒳 := {X_F^max ω^{−2}(1 + 1/2)^i} ∩ [X_F^max ω^{−2}, 16dX_F^max].
   359	    - 𝒳 has O(log ω) = O(Λ) elements.
   360	    - It is independent of t, P, ℓ and the residue.
   361	    - It contains a point x₁ ∈ (ty/4, ty/2]. Consecutive points differ by the factor 3/2 < 2, and
   362	      ty/4 ≥ tY_ω/4 ≥ X_F^max ω^{−2} for large N, since tY_ω ≥ X_F^max/(C_d 2^{2d}ω).
   363	  - Call t **good** if, for all x ∈ 𝒳 and all reduced residues r′,
   364	    |Ψ(x, B; t, r′) − Ψ_t(x, B)/φ(t)| ≤ Ψ_t(x, B)/(2φ(t)).
   365	  - For good t, (T) and (Hi) give
   366	      #{smooth terms} ≥ Ψ(x₁; t, ±r) − Ψ(|F̂|; t, ±r) − 2(|F̂|/t + 1)
   367	                      ≥ Ψ(x₁)/(4t) − 3(|F̂|/t + 1) ≥ Ψ(ty)/(16t) − 3(|F̂|/t + 1).
   368	      (The progression contains every integer ≡ ±r (mod t) with absolute value in (|F̂|, x₁].)
   369	  - Dividing by y + 1 and using ϱ(ty) ≥ ϱ(16dX_F^max)/2 ≥ ρ_F/(log)^{O(1)} (by (Hi) and (C8.16)) gives
   370	    Pr ≥ ρ_F/(log)^{O(1)} − O(ωV/H). The error term is polynomially small, since |F̂|/(tY_ω) ≪ ωV/H.
   371	- Combining (b)–(d): **if t is good, then Pr_c[F_A(P) ∈ 𝒮_B] ≥ ρ_F/(log)^{O(1)} − 8d²V/H − 4/ω ≥
   372	  ρ_F/(log)^{O(1)}.**
   373	  - Here V/H ≤ N^{−η₀}L^{O(1)} by the h-floor 1/(4k) + η₀, and 1/ω = L^{−C₁}.
   374	  - This is hypD item 4: additive errors ≪ ρ_F rather than o(1), with truncation ω ≥ L^C.
   375	
   376	**Step 3 (bad moduli are rare; hypD item 1).**
   377	- If t is not good, then for some x ∈ 𝒳 and some r′, |E(x; t, r′)| > Ψ_t(x)/(2φ(t)) ≥ Ψ(x)/(4t), by (T).
   378	- So, by Lemma 8.5′ applied at each x ∈ 𝒳 and each of the O(1) dyadic Q covering
   379	  [MV/32, 2MV]·L^{o(1)}:
   380	    #{bad t ∈ 𝒯_Q} ≤ Σ_{x∈𝒳} (4Q/Ψ(x))·CΨ(x)δ_𝒯ε_N ≪ (log ω)·ε_N·#𝒯_Q.
   381	- The hypotheses of Lemma 8.5′ hold:
   382	  - q = s + 1/(4k) + o(1) > 1/(2k) + η₁, because k > (d+1)/4;
   383	  - q < ξ/2 ⟺ h > s − (d−2)/(4k), the level-½ condition, with margin η₀.
   384	- The goodness exponent is a constant 1/2. LV's Definition 5.12 exponent 1 + o(1) is not used, so the
   385	  N^{o(1)} loss that the blind round flagged does not arise.
   386	
   387	**Step 4 (assembly; hypD items 2 and 5).**
   388	- *Good mass.* By Step 1(ii)–(iii) and (Hi) with z ≤ 64:
   389	    Σ_{n∈I_P∩𝒮_B} |𝒢_P(n)| ≥ (M/(CV log log N))·Ψ(I_P) ≥ M²ρ_r/(Cℓ log log N).
   390	- *Bad mass.* By Step 1(i), the n with ℓn bad carry total weight ≤ (2M/V)·#{bad n ≤ 2MV/ℓ}. Averaged
   391	  over ℓ, Step 3 gives
   392	    π_J^{−1} Σ_ℓ #{bad n for ℓ} ≤ (log ω)ε_N·π_J^{−1} Σ_ℓ #{n ≤ 2MV/ℓ : n ∈ 𝒮_B} ≪ (log ω)ε_N MVρ_r/ℓ.
   393	  This is o(1/log log N) times the good mass, uniformly in N.
   394	- *Total.* Summing over P ∈ 𝒫_ℓ, with #𝒫_ℓ ≍ V²/log V and |𝒢| ≍ M², and inserting Step 2:
   395	    E N ≥ (log)^{−O(1)}·(V²/ℓ)·ρ_rρ_F = S²ρ_rρ_F/(log N)^{O(1)}.
   396	- *Item 5.* Small b cannot occur, since b > V/2. Irregular lattices contain no candidates (Lemma 1.3).
   397	  The finite-interval error of sampling g in a square rather than mod ℓ is inside Step 1, because the
   398	  fibres are counted exactly. ∎
   399	
   400	*Remarks.*
   401	- **What was needed beyond hypD §4.1′.**
   402	  - Restricting b to primes in (V/2, V]. This makes every moving residue reduced and removes small b.
   403	  - The 2D field. This makes the rational side a plain interval count, (Hi), with no BV.
   404	  - A residue-free grid 𝒳 for goodness.
   405	- **The 1D design.** The same proof works for (a′_k), with Step 1 replaced by Harper's Theorem 1 for
   406	  the dense moduli family {b}. That needs √ℓS ≤ (bM/ℓ)^{1/2−η}, i.e. k ≥ (3/4)(d+1)(1 + O(η)). We do
   407	  not write it out, since (a″_k) is the design used below.
   408	- **Precision.** The loss is (log N)^{O(1)}, not merely L^{o(1)}. The losses are 1/log V from prime b,
   409	  log log factors from φ(ℓn)/ℓn, and the (log)^{O(1)} of (C8.16).
   410	  - At Horn 1 (k ≍ (log N)^{1/4}), the number of grid points, ε_N and Lemma 8.5′ change. That regime
   411	    still needs the explicit-error rewrite hypD describes.
