   506	*Remarks.*
   507	1. **Why it works in 2D and not in 1D.** In (a′_k), the rational pair (s, s′) of a pair moves on the
   508	   *line* b′s − bs′ = Δ/ℓ as m varies (hypD §4.2).
   509	   - Divisor switching then sends the problem to (e, e′) solutions of a single linear equation in a
   510	     box. That count is a Diophantine 0/1 quantity with no area main term, and the method gives
   511	     nothing.
   512	   - Averaging over (b, b′, h) does not repair this: the averaged weight is concentrated on pairs with
   513	     good rational approximations b′/b to s′/s.
   514	   - With (g₀, g₁) random, the pair fills a 2D lattice region of area ≫ perimeter·(lattice scale),
   515	     which is all the argument needs. **This is the "key simplification" the prompt asked about. It is
   516	     not a sieve bound: sieve and Rankin bounds fail at density L^{−c}, see below. It is divisor
   517	     switching, made possible by the 2D randomization.**
   518	2. **Sieve and Rankin alternatives (prompt item 3, checked).**
   519	   - Shiu and Nair–Tenenbaum upper bounds for Σ F(n)F(n + h) give only ∏_{B<p≤X}(1 − 2/p) ≍
   520	     (log B/log X)², a logarithmic saving against the needed ρ².
   521	   - A Rankin tilt n^{α−1} that recovers the Dickman exponent cannot be localized to an interval or a
   522	     line: extended to all n it is dominated by small n, and a class mod q receives mass ≥ 1 > L(χ₀)/q
   523	     once q > e^{Cu}.
   524	   - Matomäki–Radziwiłł–Tao-type averaged correlations have o(1) or log-power errors, useless at
   525	     density L^{−c}.
   526	   - Pascadi's Cor. 7.2 gives exponent 13/8 − ε < 2. Codex notes that its coefficient condition can
   527	     hold for large fixed k, but the exponent still falls short.
   528	   - So, as far as we can tell, no sieve-type upper bound with loss L^{o(1)} is available in 1D; codex
   529	     reached the same conclusion.
   530	3. **Precision.** The loss is polylogarithmic, so Theorem R also meets Horn 1's precision requirement
   531	   exp(−o(Λ/k)) for k ≍ (log N)^{1/4}. The remaining Horn-1 obstacles are FM's explicit errors and LD.
