   533	## 4. PC_alg: the kernel transfer, the remaining hypothesis, and why known tools fall short
   534	
   535	### 4.1 Lemma Kern (the 2D kernel lemma) [proved; tested]
   536	
   537	**Setup.** Let d ≥ 3, and let P ≠ P′ be primitive with |P|, |P′| ∈ [V/2, √2V] and Δ = det(P, P′) ≠ 0.
   538	Put v = v(P), v′ = v(P′), and Φ: Z^d → Z², c ↦ (⟨c, v⟩, ⟨c, v′⟩). Write P′ ≡ κP (mod Δ); κ exists
   539	because P is primitive.
   540	
   541	**Statement.**
   542	- (i) Γ := Φ(Z^d) has index |Δ| and equals {(X, X′) : X′ ≡ κ^{d−1}X (mod Δ)}.
   543	- (ii) ker Φ = Q_{PP′}·Z[x, y]_{d−3}, where Q_{PP′} = (bx − ay)(b′x − a′y). This kernel is saturated, and
   544	  its monomial basis has ‖·‖_∞ ≤ 2V² on 𝒫_ℓ, where |a|, |b| ≤ V. In general the bound is
   545	  ≤ 4V² for |P| ≤ √2V, and then H ≥ 2V² is required; only constants change.
   546	- (iii) If H ≥ V², then for every z ∈ Z²,
   547	    Pr_{c∈C_H}[Φ(c) = z] ≤ C_d·|Δ|/(H²‖v ∧ v′‖) ≤ C′_d/(H²V^{2d−4}).
   548	
   549	*Proof.*
   550	- **(ii).** ⟨c, v(P)⟩ = C(P) for the form C = Σ c_i x^{d−1−i}y^i. C(P) = 0 with P primitive gives
   551	  (bx − ay) | C over Z, by Gauss's lemma. Then C(P′) = 0 and det(P, P′) ≠ 0 give (b′x − a′y) | C/(bx − ay).
   552	  So ker Φ = Q·Z[x, y]_{d−3}. A multiple of Q that is integral is an integral multiple, again by Gauss's
   553	  lemma, so the kernel is saturated.
   554	- **(i).** The index of Φ(Z^d) is the gcd of the 2×2 minors of [v; v′], which is |Δ| (hypD Prop G(i),
   555	  (I6); also codex pcest_1, via a unimodular change sending P to (1, 0)). Since P′ ≡ κP (mod Δ),
   556	  v′ ≡ κ^{d−1}v (mod Δ), so Γ lies in the displayed lattice, which also has index |Δ|. Hence they are
   557	  equal.
   558	- **(iii).**
   559	  - A fibre is (c₀ + K) ∩ C_H, with K = ker Φ of rank d − 2 and det K = ‖v ∧ v′‖/|Δ|. Here we use that
   560	    a saturated lattice and its orthogonal complement in Z^d have the same determinant, and that the row
   561	    lattice of [v; v′] has index |Δ| in its saturation.
   562	  - The translates c + F, where F is the fundamental cell of the basis in (ii), are disjoint. They lie
   563	    in the section of the cube [−(2d−3)H, (2d−3)H]^d by a (d−2)-flat, whose volume is ≤ C_dH^{d−2}.
   564	  - So the fibre has ≤ C_dH^{d−2}|Δ|/‖v ∧ v′‖ points. Divide by (2H + 1)^d.
   565	  - For the second inequality: every 2×2 minor of [v; v′] equals Δ·W_{ij}(P, P′), with W bihomogeneous
   566	    of bidegree (d−2, d−2). For example W_{01} = (aa′)^{d−2}.
   567	  - The vector W(P, P′) of all divided minors never vanishes on (S¹)² (repaired after codex pcest_2).
   568	    - Off the diagonal and antidiagonal, this is injectivity of the projective Veronese map: v(P) ∦ v(P′)
   569	      when P ∦ P′, and Δ ≠ 0.
   570	    - On the diagonal, W(P, P) is the wedge of v(P) with its tangent ∂_u v(P) divided by det(P, u). It
   571	      is nonzero because the rational normal curve is nondegenerate. For example, at P = (1, 0) the
   572	      adjacent divided minor W_{01} equals 1. The antidiagonal is the same up to sign.
   573	    - W is continuous, so by compactness ‖v ∧ v′‖ ≥ c_d|Δ|(|P||P′|)^{d−2}. ∎
   574	    - **Effective version for candidates (Opus proof critic).** The minor (d−2, d−1) equals
   575	      (bb′)^{d−2}Δ. On 𝒫_ℓ we have b, b′ > V/2, so ‖v ∧ v′‖ ≥ |Δ|(V/2)^{2d−4} directly, without any
   576	      compactness argument. This is all that Prop 4.2 uses.
   577	    - The exact identity det K·|Δ| = ‖v ∧ v′‖ and the bound with c_d > 0.05 (d ≤ 7) are checked exactly
   578	      in `tests/test_pcest.py`.
   579	
   580	*Cost [proved arithmetic].*
   581	- H ≥ V² = ℓS² means h > 1/(2k), which raises the h-floor from LV's 1/(4k) (FM) to 1/(2k).
   582	- Then θ = 2s + (d−1)/(4k) + max(1/(2k), s − (d−2)/(4k)). The floor binds only for k < d(d+1)/4,
   583	  against (d² − 1)/4 before.
   584	- **The infimum over k is unchanged.**
   585	- Finite-k examples: d = 9, k = 20 gives θ = 0.325 instead of 0.3125, so c = 0.937 instead of 0.928. At
   586	  d = 9 the minimal k with c < 1 moves from 11 to 12 (`src/pcest/params.py`). Codex's (5) agrees.
   587	- If one also wanted the moduli G|Δ to stay below |F|^{1/2} (not needed below), the condition would
   588	  become h > s + (6 − d)/(4k) (codex).
   589	
   590	### 4.2 Reduction of PC to one arithmetic hypothesis
   591	
   592	**Notation.** For a pair (P, P′) ∈ 𝒫_ℓ² with ℓ | Δ, and g ∈ 𝒢 ∩ L_ℓ(P):
   593	- G = G_g(P), G′ = G_g(P′), and T := dHV^{d−1};
   594	- the full windows I := F̂_g(P) + G·[−T, T] and I′ likewise;
   595	- the lift classes r_j := F̂_g(P) + Gj (mod G|Δ|) and r′_j := F̂_g(P′) + G′κ^{d−1}j (mod G′|Δ|), for
   596	  j mod |Δ|.
   597	
   598	**The normalized lift correlation.**
   599	  κ_alg(ℓ, P, P′, g) := |Δ|·Σ_{j mod |Δ|} Ψ_I(G|Δ|, r_j)·Ψ_{I′}(G′|Δ|, r′_j) / (4T²·ϱ(T|G|, B)·ϱ(T|G′|, B)),
   600	where Ψ_I(m, r) := #{F ∈ I ∩ 𝒮_B : F ≡ r (mod m)}. If the smooth numbers of each window were spread
   601	evenly over the |Δ| lift classes, κ_alg would be ≍ 1.
   602	
   603	**Prop 4.2 [proved].** Assume h ≥ 1/(2k) + η₀. For each such (ℓ, P, P′, g),
   604	  Pr_c[F_A(P), F_A(P′) ∈ 𝒮_B] ≤ C_d·(V²/|Δ|)·ϱ(T|G|)ϱ(T|G′|)·κ_alg.
   605	*Proof.*
   606	- By Lemma Kern(iii), Pr_c ≤ C′_d(H²V^{2d−4})^{−1}·#{z ∈ Γ ∩ [−T, T]² : F̂ + Gz₁ and F̂′ + G′z₂ smooth}.
   607	- By Lemma Kern(i), that count is Σ_j Ψ_I(G|Δ|, r_j)Ψ_{I′}(G′|Δ|, r′_j).
   608	- Insert the definition of κ_alg and T² = d²H²V^{2d−2}. ∎
   609	
   610	Here V²/|Δ| = S²/q is comparable to (not equal to) the bounding-box factor 1/sin∠(P, P′).
   611	- If κ_alg were ≤ L^{o(1)}, the factor would cost only O(log S) on average. For fixed P and q, the
   612	  equation det(P, P′) = ±ℓq has O(1) solutions P′ in the box (they lie on two lines parallel to P,
   613	  spaced by |P| ≥ V/2).
   614	- So Σ over pairs of (S²/q)·(Theorem R's uniform per-pair bound) loses only a harmonic sum
   615	  Σ_{q≤2S²} 1/q.
   616	- LD keeps the product (S²/q)·κ_alg; the two factors are not separated.
   617	
   618	**Hypothesis LD (lift dispersion under rational-smoothness weights) [open].** Uniformly in N:
   619	  E_ℓ Σ_{P≠P′, ℓ|Δ} |𝒢|^{−1} Σ_{g∈𝒢∩L_ℓ(P)} Y_PY_{P′}·(S²/q)·κ_alg ≤ L^{o(1)}·E_ℓ Σ_{P≠P′, ℓ|Δ} |𝒢|^{−1} Σ_g Y_PY_{P′}.
   620	- It is an upper bound, at precision L^{o(1)}, for a restricted second moment of smooth numbers in the
   621	  lift classes of moving residues to moduli G|Δ| = ℓ²nq.
   622	- The weights are the actual rational-smooth multiplicities. Theorem R shows that the right side is
   623	  ≤ (log N)^{O(1)}S⁴ρ_r².
   624	- LD is implied by a suitably weighted form of hypD's RBDH′ (an o(1) relative variance), but it is
   625	  *weaker*: it is an upper bound within L^{o(1)}. It is codex pcest_1's (6) with the kernel transfer
   626	  carried out. We do not claim an equivalence.
   627	
   628	**Theorem 4.3 [proved implication; repaired after codex pcest_2].**
   629	- **Hypotheses.** Those of Theorem FM, with h ≥ 1/(2k) + η₀ and S² := (log N)^{C₃}/(ρ_rρ_F), where C₃ is
   630	  larger than the polylog exponent lost in Theorem FM. Let N_ℓ(A) be the **filtered** count of §1
   631	  (|G(P)| ≥ MV/32).
   632	- **Conclusions.**
   633	  - (i) FM + Theorem R + Prop 4.2 + LD ⟹ PC := E[N(N−1)]/(E N)² ≤ L^{o(1)}.
   634	  - (ii) Hence, by Prop PZ (E N ≥ 1), D_k(γ) holds with γ ≥ L^{−o(1)}.
   635	*Proof.*
   636	- E[N(N−1)] ≤ E_ℓ Σ_{P≠P′, ℓ|Δ} |𝒢|^{−1} Σ_{g: |G|,|G′| ≥ MV/32} Y_PY_{P′} Pr_c[·].
   637	- By the filter, T|G| ∈ [TMV/32, 2TMV], so ϱ(T|G|) ≤ 64ϱ(TMV) ≤ ρ_F(log N)^{O(1)} by (Hi) and (C8.16).
   638	  - The unfiltered version of this step was wrong: for |G| ≈ ℓ, the ratio X_F/(T|G|) is a positive
   639	    power of N.
   640	- By positivity, LD (stated over all g) bounds the filtered sum.
   641	- So, by Prop 4.2, LD and Theorem R, E[N(N−1)] ≤ C_dρ_F²·L^{o(1)}·S⁴ρ_r²(log N)^{O(1)}.
   642	- By Theorem FM, which only counts |G| ∈ [MV/32, 2MV] and so applies to the filtered count,
   643	  (E N)² ≥ S⁴ρ_r²ρ_F²/(log)^{O(1)} and E N ≥ 1.
   644	- Theorem R's condition S² ≥ ρ_r^{−2} holds for large N, since ρ_F/ρ_r = L^{−c+o(1)} for some c > 0. ∎
   645	- (The first version took S² = L^ε/(ρ_rρ_F), giving L^{O(ε)} losses that are not L^{o(1)}. The
   646	  polylogarithmic choice of S² fixes this.)
   647	
   648	### 4.3 Why LD is not available, and what each known tool gives
   649	
   650	All items below are statements about what the cited tools give by themselves. They are not lower bounds
   651	on LD, and they do not exclude proofs that combine these tools with further arithmetic information.
   652	
   653	- **(B1′) Naive use of Harper's BDH (all residues, arbitrary residue) loses the factor G.** For one
   654	  modulus m = G|Δ|:
   655	  - LD needs the |Δ| lift classes, with main-term mass |Δ|(Ψ/m)² = Ψ²/(G·m).
   656	  - Harper's Theorem 2 (Q ≤ Ψ) controls Σ_{m≤Q} Σ_{all reduced r}|E(m, r)|², a global sum whose
   657	    per-modulus scale is Ψ²/m.
   658	  - Harper's asymptotic BDH for general sequences (J. London Math. Soc. 112 (2025) e70293,
   659	    arXiv 2412.19644, Cor. 1) confirms that the all-residue variance is dominated by its diagonal QΨ.
   660	    It has no restricted-moduli or restricted-residue form either.
   661	  - Positivity cannot recover the factor G = N^{s+1/(4k)+o(1)}, and e^{−cu/log²u} cannot compensate a
   662	    polynomial factor (codex pcest_1, Q2). The modulus family is also sparse, with density
   663	    ≈ ρ_r/(ℓ log Y), so restricted versions are needed anyway, as in barrier B1.
   664	- **(B2′) Divisor switching needs small moduli.** Lemma DS works because q ≤ y^{O(1)}.
   665	  - Applied to F in a progression mod G ≈ N^{s+1/(4k)}, the complementary-factor count is only
   666	    nontrivial when the divisor cutoff is D ≲ X_F/G.
   667	  - The divisor sum is then evaluated at density ϱ(X_F/(Gy)) instead of ϱ(X_F), a loss of L^{Θ(1)}.
   668	  - The q-part of the coupling (modulus q ≤ 2S²) is small. But it sits inside an AP to the large
   669	    modulus G, so the same obstruction applies.
   670	- **(B3′) The arithmetic large sieve loses 1/(ρ_Fδ) [assessment].** Use the large sieve in its standard
   671	  form, over primitive characters, or over primes p ≤ Q with Σ_p p Σ_w |Z(p, w) − Z/p|² ≤ (x + Q²)Z for
   672	  general moduli via induced-character multiplicities. It is insensitive to the sparseness of smooth
   673	  numbers.
   674	  - Its diagonal term x·Ψ exceeds the needed scale by the factor 1/(ρ_Fδ) = L^{Θ(1)}, where δ is the
   675	    density of the modulus family.
   676	  - The same phenomenon is why Fouvry–Tenenbaum's large-sieve BV works only for y ≥ exp(log^{2/3+ε}x)
   677	    (Harper's introduction).
   678	- **Prop B5 (an L¹ saving alone does not give L² control) [proved; elementary; scope narrowed after
   679	  codex pcest_2].**
   680	  - The max-residue estimates we know are L¹ bounds over moduli of β − 1, with saving
   681	    ε_N = H(u)^{−c} + …. These are Harper Thm 1, LV Lemma 8.5 and our Lemma 8.5′, and, with fixed
   682	    residues, de la Bretèche–Drappeau, Drappeau and Pascadi.
   683	  - The proof of LD from β-information needs an L² bound: κ_alg ≤ β(G|Δ)·β(G′|Δ)-type products, and
   684	    neither factor is controlled pointwise.
   685	  - Example: let β = T with probability ε_N/T, and β = 1 otherwise, with T ≤ 1/ρ_F (the trivial range).
   686	    The L¹ excess is ε_N, but the L² excess is ε_NT, which reaches ε_N/ρ_F = L^{Θ(1)}.
   687	  - So no inequality of the form "L¹ saving ⟹ L² control" holds at these densities in general.
   688	  - A proof of LD that goes through max-class ratios needs, in addition, a *large-values* estimate: the
   689	    moduli with an overloaded lift class must have weighted measure ≪ L^{o(1)}T^{−2}, for T up to
   690	    1/ρ_F.
   691	  - This does not show that every LD proof must take this form.
   692	- **Prop Z (the ℓ-part is one shared random variable) [proved; observation].**
   693	  - For P ∈ Λ_ℓ(g), P ≡ (b/g₁)(g₀, g₁) (mod ℓ). Hence ⟨c, v(P)⟩ ≡ (b/g₁)^{d−1}R_c(g₀, g₁) (mod ℓ).
   694	  - So the ℓ-adic lift of every candidate F_A(P) mod ℓ|G| is determined by one field constant
   695	    ζ := R_c(g₀, g₁)g₁^{1−d} (mod ℓ), through ζb^{d−1}.
   696	  - Conditioning PZ on ζ removes the ℓ-part of the coupling. But it then needs anti-concentration of
   697	    E[N | ζ] in ζ. That is again a lift-equidistribution statement of LD's type [assessment; not a
   698	    proved equivalence].
   699	  - It does localize the difficulty: equidistribution of smooth numbers in an AP mod ℓn over the ℓ
   700	    lifts mod ℓ²n, jointly with a mod-q coupling.
   701	- **A plausible route to LD (Opus proof critic; not carried out) [assessment].**
   702	  - B1′ and B5 treat the residue as arbitrary. But by Lemma 1.2 the moving residue is explicit:
   703	    r₀ ≡ N b^d g₁^{−d} (mod G).
   704	  - At a fixed modulus t, there are about t/log V pairs (P, g), and the fractions b/g₁ mod t come from a
   705	    box of area about t. So the coarse classes are spread over a coset of the d-th powers, of index
   706	    ≤ d^{ω(t)} = L^{o(1)} on average.
   707	  - LD would then follow from two pieces:
   708	    - (i) an all-residue L² restricted BDH for the moduli ℓ²nq, with LV-type relative saving ϱ(Q). This
   709	      is the L² analogue of LV's majorant argument, Σ φ^{−1} Σ |Ψ(χ)|², which LV say their method
   710	      "naturally provides", and Lemma 8.5′'s character factorization should carry over;

 succeeded in 0ms:
   711	    - (ii) a large-values bound for the structured residues r ≡ N(b₀/g₀)^d with small b₀, g₀, where
   712	      the representation count is ≈ K on a set of measure ≈ 1/K. This is where a real obstruction
   713	      remains.
   714	  - Heuristically LD is plausible. The obvious correlation, shared divisibility by p | q, costs
   715	    ∏_{p|q}(1 + p^{1−2α}) = L^{o(1)}. E1's PC_alg of 1.05–1.27 is consistent with it.
   716	- **Two algebraic sides (codex).** Independent fields do not decorrelate two evaluations in one field.
   717	  The formal constant worsens to (1 + 4/(d+1))/√2, and an analogue of LD is still needed.
   718	
   719	**Sufficient single-family form [outline, not a proved reduction].**
   720	- By Cauchy–Schwarz over j, by bounding the moving class by its maximum over classes mod G (which
   721	  depends only on n), and by Lemma DS in the n′ variable (Π's long side makes an angle in
   722	  [atan ½, atan 2] with the axes), LD would follow from LV*:
   723	    Σ_{n∈(X′, 2X′], n∈𝒮_B} max_{(r,ℓn)=1} |Δ|·Σ_{j mod |Δ|} Ψ_I(ℓn|Δ|, r + ℓnj)² / (2Tϱ_F)² ≤ L^{o(1)}·Ψ(2X′, B)
   724	  on average over ℓ ∈ J and |Δ| = ℓq with weight 1/q, for X′ ∈ [X_rS^{−2}, X_r].
   725	- LV* is a "max over coarse classes, L² over the fine lifts" statement for the single family {ℓ²nq}.
   726	  We have not written this reduction out in full.
   727	
   728	## 5. Assembly: the sharpest conditional theorem
   729	
   730	**Assumption (LV6) [open; inherited from lemmaM Corollary DD and hypD Corollary K].** LV §6
   731	(characters, square roots, the residual square-class kernel) has to be adapted to:
   732	- pure-descent rows from pairs of height ≤ V = N^{1/(4k)}L^{O(1)};
   733	- the non-monic pair (f, g₁x − g₀);
   734	- the one extra g₁-parity column.
   735	The adaptation must keep the cost (rows)^{2+o(1)} and the transcript invariance (T) of Theorem DD.
   736	LV's Theorem 2.6 is written for heights L(1/3). The adaptation has not been written out, by lemmaM,
   737	hypD or us.
   738	
   739	**Theorem A [conditional on LD and LV6].**
   740	- A possibly cheaper alternative to A′, not checked: Bernstein's deterministic batch smoothness
   741	  algorithm, run over batches of ≥ B/polylog candidates gathered across R-trials and fields, might
   742	  remove HECM at no cost to the exponent (Opus proof critic).
   743	- **Statement.**
   744	  - Fix odd d ≥ 3, k > (d+1)/4, η₀ > 0, and let θ := θ(d, k) = 2s + (d−1)/(4k) + max(1/(2k),
   745	    s − (d−2)/(4k)) with θ < 1.
   746	  - Let h be the corresponding height plus η₀, let a := θ/√2, and B = L[1/2, a].
   747	  - Assume LD for design (a″_k) at these parameters, uniformly in N, and LV6.
   748	  - Then every N is factored in expected time L_N[1/2, (1+θ)/√2 + O(η₀) + o(1)].
   749	- **Consequences.** Infimum over k: (1 + 3/(d+1))/√2, i.e. 0.9723 (d = 7), 0.9192 (d = 9), 0.8839
   750	  (d = 11) and 0.8132 (d = 19). The constant is < 1 iff d ≥ 7, with k ≥ 13 (d = 7) or k ≥ 12 (d = 9).
   751	- **Further explicit assumptions (codex pcest_2).**
   752	  - (HECM) LPP's running-time theorem for the hyperelliptic curve method, as used by LV Cor. 3.19. The
   753	    published proof trail is incomplete (LPP II defers details to an unpublished Part III); Theorem A′
   754	    removes this assumption.
   755	  - Reducible f are rejected. The irreducibility transfer omits the O(log N) primes dividing g₁N; the
   756	    exceptional probability is polynomially small.
   757	  - The g₁ column records the parity of the number of atomic special-q relations in an assembled row,
   758	    counted with multiplicity.
   759	- **Ingredients and their status.**
   760	  - Proved: Lemma GB, Theorem FM, Theorem R, Lemma Kern, Prop 4.2, Theorem 4.3.
   761	  - From hypD and lemmaM: Theorems DD, S′, K and Corollary K's algorithm.
   762	  - Prior art: Lenstra–Pomerance as the shape test, and HECM for the B-smoothness tests (caveat below).
   763	
   764	*Proof.*
   765	1. Preprocess by trial division to B.
   766	2. Theorem 4.3 gives D_k(γ) with γ ≥ L^{−o(1)}.
   767	3. hypD Theorem K(i)–(iii) and its field-selection rule give pure-descent rows at expected cost
   768	   (T_shape + T_SQ)·L^{o(1)} each, with T_shape = L_N[1/2, 1/√2] (LP on √N-size integers) and
   769	   T_SQ = S²L^{o(1)} = L^{θ/(2a)+o(1)}.
   770	4. B·L^{o(1)} rows, LV6 and Theorem DD give a nontrivial factor with probability ≥ 1/2 per dependency.
   771	5. Cost: L[1/2, a + max(1/√2, θ/(2a))] + L[1/2, 2a]. At a = θ/√2 this is L[1/2, (1+θ)/√2], since
   772	   θ ≤ 1. ∎
   773	
   774	**Theorem A′ (no HECM) [conditional on LD and LV6].**
   775	- Replace HECM by the deterministic Pollard–Strassen method, which finds all prime factors ≤ B of m in
   776	  B^{1/2+o(1)}(log m)^{O(1)} time (Pollard, Proc. Camb. Phil. Soc. 76 (1974); Strassen, Jber. DMV 78
   777	  (1976); Costa–Harvey, Math. Comp. 83 (2014)).
   778	- Each smoothness test then costs L^{a/2}, and
   779	    c_PS = min_a max(2a, a + max(1/√2, θ/(2a) + a/2)).
   780	- For θ ≤ 1/2 the optimum is at a² − √2a + θ = 0, giving c_PS = 1/√2 + (√2 − √(2 − 4θ))/2.
   781	- θ → 3/(d+1) gives d = 7: 1.0607; d = 9: 0.9670 (a = 0.2599); d = 11: 0.9142; d = 19: 0.8226. These
   782	  were corrected after codex pcest_2; the first version printed 0.9671 and 0.9144 from a grid search.
   783	- So **the HECM caveat (LPP Part III unpublished) can be removed at a cost, and c < 1 survives for
   784	  d ≥ 9**.
   785	- A possibly cheaper route, not checked: Pomerance's (1987) rigorous ECM detection of *most* smooth
   786	  numbers. Whether a "most smooth values" guarantee suffices for FM and PZ (literature critic) is open. LV6 might itself use HECM; if so, the same replacement applies there at L^{o(1)}-many tests
   787	  per row. We did not check LV §6 for this.
   788	
   789	**What exactly is missing for a theorem beating Lenstra–Pomerance [the sharpest form we can state].**
   790	1. **LD** (§4.2): a restricted, rational-smoothness-weighted BDH upper bound at precision L^{o(1)} for
   791	   smooth numbers in the |Δ| lift classes of a reduced residue to moduli ℓ²nq. Here n is B-smooth,
   792	   q ≤ 2S², the moduli are ≍ N^{s+3/(4k)}L^{O(1)}, and the values are ≍ N^{s+h+d/(4k)}.
   793	2. **LV6** (bookkeeping of a known method at new heights; not expected to need a new idea, but not
   794	   written).
   795	
   796	Within this project's proved results, nothing else is missing. FM, the binary rational correlation,
   797	the kernel transfer, the split counting (Theorem S′), the complete-target-list reduction (Theorem K)
   798	and fruitfulness (DD) are proved here or in hypD/lemmaM.
   799	
   800	Caveats:
   801	- these are internal, unrefereed results;
   802	- the HECM running-time theorem (Theorem A only; A′ avoids it);
   803	- the Lemma 5.1 irreducibility transfer, checked at the level of statements.
   804	
   805	**Has anyone beaten c = 1 rigorously (1992–2026)?** To our knowledge, no. Codex pcest_1 and pcest_2 and
   806	the Opus literature critic (§7) all searched. Neither found a rigorous algorithm factoring every N in L[1/2, c] with c < 1. The
   807	rigorous record remains Lenstra–Pomerance 1992 (JAMS 5, 483–516).
   808	- Related results are either heuristic (NFS; Stange 2022) or concern other problems:
   809	  - Kleinjung–Wesolowski: discrete logarithms in finite fields of fixed characteristic (JAMS 35, 2022).
   810	  - de Boer–Pellet-Mary–Wesolowski (arXiv 2512.01588): ERH, class and unit groups.
   811	- Under GRH the best rigorous constant is also c = 1: Seysen and A. K. Lenstra's class-group method,
   812	  whose GRH dependence Lenstra–Pomerance removed. We know of no c < 1 under a standard hypothesis.
   813	- Lee–Venkatesan (J. Number Theory 187 (2018) 92–159) produce congruences in L[1/3], but their
   814	  factoring conclusion is
   815	  conditional on their Conjecture 7.1.
   816	- This is a search result, not a certification.
